 2. 🗳️ Swing state debate night 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.) points at his challenger, Rep. Mike Collins (R-Ga.), during their debate in Atlanta last night. Photo: Brian Cahn/Zuma via Reuters Axios' Zachary Basu, Mike Zapler and Stef W. Kight — tracking a big midterm debate night — write: 

 Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.) delivered another debate performance tailor-made for social media last night, feeding the presidential speculation he insists is entirely misplaced.

 Ossoff emphatically ruled out a White House bid. Asked whether he'd pledge to serve a full six-year Senate term if reelected, Ossoff said : "Yes, I will. I have zero interest in running for president. I'm not running for president. I'm running for reelection to the Senate." Republican challenger Rep. Mike Collins countered: "He is running. He's going to be your DSA candidate." Ossoff is not a Democratic Socialists of America member. The big picture: Georgia was one of three battleground states hosting Senate debates last night. Candidates in Iowa and Maine also clashed over President Trump's foreign policy, the cost of living and their records in Washington.

 🍑 In Georgia, Ossoff and Collins traded vicious personal attacks throughout their second and final debate, reprising a feud that has become a viral spectacle online.

 "Will you commit tonight that for the remainder of this campaign — it's just three weeks, Mike — you won't allow any more neo-Nazi propaganda to be produced from your house?" Ossoff demanded , referring to Collins' son-in-law, an alleged white nationalist influencer. Collins accused Ossoff of inciting threats against his family, then attacked him over campaign support from billionaire Reid Hoffman. 🦞 In Maine, Republican Sen. Susan Collins cast her independence from Trump as a political asset, arguing that her willingness to challenge the president — and work with him when necessary — has delivered for her state.

 Democratic challenger Troy Jackson argued that Collins has spent years helping Trump advance policies that hurt Maine, only to express "concern" and claim credit for mitigating the damage. 🌽 In Iowa, GOP Rep. Ashley Hinson and Democratic challenger Josh Turek squared off in a race that has Republicans on edge , as Trump's tariffs and soaring farm costs threaten their grip on a state he carried by 13 points.

 Hinson broke with Trump over his deal to ease sanctions on Russian diesel , saying she believed there were ways to lower fuel prices without empowering Vladimir Putin and his war in Ukraine. Turek accused Hinson of helping drive Iowa toward a "second farm crisis" through her support for Trump's tariffs and votes against congressional efforts to end the Iran war. More Georgia takeaways ... Share this story . 

 
 
