 5. 🔎 How to get caught spying 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Justice Department via Reuters Above is what the Justice Department says is a photo of Abdulrahman Alfozan, 35, a Saudi national who was then an OpenAI engineer, peering behind a TV last week in an OpenAI conference room in San Francisco. Prosecutors say the man was checking a surreptitious audio recording device that had earlier been inserted in a USB port on the back of the TV.

 Alfozan, who joined OpenAI in July 2025, was arrested Friday and charged with installing the encrypted device. He was fired and is in federal custody . The device, installed on or about Sept. 28, had been concealed in one of the lowest-tech ways possible: wrapped in toilet paper.

 Prosecutors say that on the same day this photo was taken, Alfozan "left a bag unattended in a different room in an OpenAI building. OpenAI security personnel found and opened Alfozan's bag, inside which they discovered — among other things, including Alfozan's wallet and ID — another recording device" similar to the one behind the TV. Photo: Justice Department via Reuters The Justice Department account says security footage showed Alfozan "near the executive conference room on multiple dates prior to the device's discovery, although he had no authority to access or to participate in meetings occurring in the executive conference room."

 Meetings in the room "frequently involved discussions of highly confidential technical, commercial, and strategic information," the government says. Prosecutors say a chatbot account associated with Alfozan's spouse "submitted prompts between Sept. 24 and Sept. 30 related to building, customizing, installing, audio recording, testing, obfuscating, and remote accessing a device." OpenAI announced on X: "We used our models to understand the device and where it had come from, identifying that a specific employee had planted it a few days earlier. ... At this time, we have no indications of this individual inappropriately accessing or exfiltrating model weights, source code, or customer data. The employee was terminated."

 Alfozan appeared in federal court yesterday on a charge of using or endeavoring to use an electronic, mechanical, or other device to intercept oral communications. It wasn't immediately clear whether he has a lawyer to speak on his behalf. Alfozan is due back in court next Monday. Get the latest . 

 
 
